    {
      "id": "Gj2ltGFCcPCQHd131ENsq",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 190.9406937672196,
      "y": 2342.4137301859673,
      "width": 1114.7193603515625,
      "height": 725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2B",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 810862026,
      "version": 1591,
      "versionNonce": 741936982,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781728837756,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "-- determine which user has control (has the floor) and how to take turns when multiple people access a resource.\n-- Token based: a token circulates among participants, only the participant holding the token can make changes.\nParticipant can request a token and the predefined policy (first-come-first-served or free-for-all) determines \nif request is granted\n-- Chair-controlled: a designated chairperson and moderator grants and revokes participant access to resources\n-- Lock model: prevent unwanted access to data (read/or write)\n---> can be done at document/paragraph/sentence/word level \n---> lock can be requested in both optimistic and pessimistic fashions\n--- pessimistic: client make a network request to obtain the lock and only modify after the lock is granted.\nin this case, user will experience delay before performing any action\n--- optimisitic: client allow modification and request the lock at the same time. If lock is rejected,\nthen client will rollback the updates made prior to requesting the lock\n-- Lock release: Determine idle threshold can be tricky. Similar to floor control model, allow users \ntake control over lock anytime they want. \n\n\n -- A transaction-based approach is an optimistic method where users make changes locally \nand changes are validated at the end of the transaction, \nsimilar to distributed version control systems like Git and Mercurial. \nVersion control systems manage changes to documents or resources\n by maintaining different versions and allowing users to merge changes.\n-- Change happen locally and not pushed immediately\n-- Benefit of zero latency, however, updates is not real time\n\n\n\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "-- determine which user has control (has the floor) and how to take turns when multiple people access a resource.\n-- Token based: a token circulates among participants, only the participant holding the token can make changes.\nParticipant can request a token and the predefined policy (first-come-first-served or free-for-all) determines \nif request is granted\n-- Chair-controlled: a designated chairperson and moderator grants and revokes participant access to resources\n-- Lock model: prevent unwanted access to data (read/or write)\n---> can be done at document/paragraph/sentence/word level \n---> lock can be requested in both optimistic and pessimistic fashions\n--- pessimistic: client make a network request to obtain the lock and only modify after the lock is granted.\nin this case, user will experience delay before performing any action\n--- optimisitic: client allow modification and request the lock at the same time. If lock is rejected,\nthen client will rollback the updates made prior to requesting the lock\n-- Lock release: Determine idle threshold can be tricky. Similar to floor control model, allow users \ntake control over lock anytime they want. \n\n\n -- A transaction-based approach is an optimistic method where users make changes locally \nand changes are validated at the end of the transaction, \nsimilar to distributed version control systems like Git and Mercurial. \nVersion control systems manage changes to documents or resources\n by maintaining different versions and allowing users to merge changes.\n-- Change happen locally and not pushed immediately\n-- Benefit of zero latency, however, updates is not real time\n\n\n\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
