      "updated": 1781727715583,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "**** Transaction based model",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "**** Transaction based model",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "3ucPq5oTtcw01LjNRAFPv",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 140.98159780531898,
      "y": 2933.168444794784,
      "width": 1410.419189453125,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2E",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 529073942,
      "version": 877,
      "versionNonce": 2128305674,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781729605558,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "**** Version detection Model ===> use this for document update\nEach update request contains the new data and the document revision the change was acted on. When the server receives the update request, \nit first checks if the revision in the request is the same as the current revision. \n===> If the revisions match, the server updates the document, \nsaves it as a new revision, and broadcasts this information to all peers so that everyone is on the latest revision. \n===> If they are different, then there's a \"version mismatch\", either reject or compensate the cahnge\n-- document is replicated on machines of all participants for optimistic update\n-- Optimistic update and no-blocking: update will be made locally without worrying about conflicts.\nBenefit for zero latency responsiveness during local update, and not rely of speed of network vs, how fast we type\n-- No locking: available to every participant all the time\n-- Automatic conflict resolution: conflict detect on the server, compensating changes inform the client \n(operational transformation and CRDT-- conflict free replicated data type)",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "**** Version detection Model ===> use this for document update\nEach update request contains the new data and the document revision the change was acted on. When the server receives the update request, \nit first checks if the revision in the request is the same as the current revision. \n===> If the revisions match, the server updates the document, \nsaves it as a new revision, and broadcasts this information to all peers so that everyone is on the latest revision. \n===> If they are different, then there's a \"version mismatch\", either reject or compensate the cahnge\n-- document is replicated on machines of all participants for optimistic update\n-- Optimistic update and no-blocking: update will be made locally without worrying about conflicts.\nBenefit for zero latency responsiveness during local update, and not rely of speed of network vs, how fast we type\n-- No locking: available to every participant all the time\n-- Automatic conflict resolution: conflict detect on the server, compensating changes inform the client \n(operational transformation and CRDT-- conflict free replicated data type)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "tPf1rALs7UejmWhKJQo-D",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 149.52648446770462,
      "y": 3245.5424535688094,
      "width": 1202.5789794921875,
      "height": 450,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#f08c00",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2F",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 176670678,
      "version": 680,
      "versionNonce": 186958614,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781730169783,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Operational Transformations (OT): \n-- OT accounts for context at the point of editing and transforms the operations accordingly\n (e.g. by modifying the offsets of insertions/deletions). Include 3 keys component\n----- Operation: basic action that users perform (inserting/deleting, modifying text)\n----- Transformation functions: determine how to adjust operation when there is conflict\n----- Control algorithm: manage sequence and context of transformations\nConflict-free Replicated Data Types (CRDTs): \n-- CRDTs enforce the use of data structures where updates are commutative \nand associative such that the order of operations does not matter.\n------ Metadata overhead\n------ Ever-increasing size\n------ Automatic conflict resolution might not always align with desired application semantics, leading to unexpected results\n====> Both CRDTs and OT can be used to implement collaborative editors. \nCRDTs are general purpose while OT has its roots in document editing. While CRDTs are newer and more trendy, \nOT is mature and excels in real-time collaborative editing applications where low latency, immediate feedback, \nand fine-grained control over user intentions are critical.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Operational Transformations (OT): \n-- OT accounts for context at the point of editing and transforms the operations accordingly\n (e.g. by modifying the offsets of insertions/deletions). Include 3 keys component\n----- Operation: basic action that users perform (inserting/deleting, modifying text)\n----- Transformation functions: determine how to adjust operation when there is conflict\n----- Control algorithm: manage sequence and context of transformations\nConflict-free Replicated Data Types (CRDTs): \n-- CRDTs enforce the use of data structures where updates are commutative \nand associative such that the order of operations does not matter.\n------ Metadata overhead\n------ Ever-increasing size\n------ Automatic conflict resolution might not always align with desired application semantics, leading to unexpected results\n====> Both CRDTs and OT can be used to implement collaborative editors. \nCRDTs are general purpose while OT has its roots in document editing. While CRDTs are newer and more trendy, \nOT is mature and excels in real-time collaborative editing applications where low latency, immediate feedback, \nand fine-grained control over user intentions are critical.\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "tavjpyKNBZEvsOQWOASXe",
      "type": "text",
